            {
              "id": "glog-399dfde8-45ab-4fa8-9293-d72bc3091cdd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. The function takes an integer argument which specifies the number of bytes to allocate. If the argument is not properly validated, it can lead to various issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the input to `malloc`. Make sure it is within the expected range and is not a negative number.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns NULL, it means that the memory allocation failed. This should be properly handled to prevent null pointer dereferencing.\n3. Always free the memory allocated by `malloc` when it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        // Invalid size\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Memory allocation failed\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the size argument is checked before calling `malloc`. The return value of `malloc` is also checked and handled properly.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-d5a85b8f-ae53-470c-9aaa-b13b968edae5",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used correctly. The function takes an integer argument which specifies the number of bytes to allocate. If the argument is not properly validated, it can lead to various issues such as buffer overflow, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the input to `malloc`. Make sure it is within the expected range and is not a negative number.\n2. Check the return value of `malloc`. If it returns NULL, it means that the memory allocation failed. This should be properly handled to prevent null pointer dereferencing.\n3. Always free the memory allocated by `malloc` when it is no longer needed to prevent memory leaks.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t size) {\n    if (size <= 0) {\n        // Invalid size\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Memory allocation failed\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the size argument is checked before calling `malloc`. The return value of `malloc` is also checked and handled properly.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
