              "id": "glog-4cfe800a-41d1-4fd3-bb83-2c170a27b9ef",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function does not check for the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source is larger than the destination. This is a common vulnerability in C++ programming that can lead to serious security issues such as code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always ensure that the destination buffer is large enough to hold the data being copied. This can be done by checking the size of the source and destination before calling `memcpy`. Alternatively, consider using safer functions that include size checks, such as `strncpy` or `memcpy_s` (in C11).\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `memcpy` function with a safer alternative that includes size checks. For example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <algorithm>\n\nstd::copy(a, a + sizeof(T), seq_a_.data());\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<algorithm>`: for the `std::copy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-120)](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
