              "id": "glog-926083f0-c186-48e9-9333-acbbca4ce546",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This is known as the \"Detected Use of Prohibited C Function (strcpy)\" vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string copy functions that include a length parameter, such as `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. These functions allow you to specify the maximum number of characters to be copied, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function like `strncpy`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[50];\nstrncpy(name, \"hipblaslt-bench\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` will copy at most `sizeof(name)` characters from the source string to the destination buffer. If the source string is shorter than this size, the remaining characters in the destination buffer will be filled with null bytes. The last line ensures that the string is null-terminated, which is necessary because `strncpy` does not null-terminate the string if the source string is longer than or equal to the size parameter.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library to execute properly:\n\n- `<cstring>`: This library provides the `strncpy` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
