              "id": "glog-3b7dadbf-b419-4408-a2ef-690c08d23e48",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(val_type_size * val_size)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory of the specified size. It returns a pointer of type void which can be cast into a pointer of any form. A vulnerability can occur when the multiplication `val_type_size * val_size` results in an integer overflow, leading to a smaller block of memory being allocated than expected. This can lead to buffer overflow attacks if the program later writes more data to the allocated memory than it can hold.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if the multiplication `val_type_size * val_size` overflows. This can be done by comparing the result of the multiplication with the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the result. If the multiplication overflows, the program should not proceed with the memory allocation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for integer overflow before calling `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (val_size != 0 && val_type_size > INT_MAX / val_size) {\n    // handle overflow, e.g., by returning an error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(val_type_size * val_size);\n    // continue with the program\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: This header defines constants with the limits of fundamental integral types for the specific system and compiler implementation used.\n- `<cstdlib>`: This header defines several general purpose functions, including dynamic memory management, random number generation, communication with the environment, integer arithmetics, searching, sorting and converting.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
