              "id": "glog-64eae5cc-f464-4087-b52c-ba4d88558e73",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the multiplication of `ptr_type_size` and `ptr_size` results in an integer overflow, the allocated memory block will be smaller than expected. This can lead to buffer overflow attacks, where an attacker can overwrite data in memory, potentially leading to arbitrary code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. If an overflow is detected, the program should handle it gracefully, for example by returning an error or by limiting the size of the allocation to a safe maximum.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (ptr_size != 0 && ptr_type_size > INT_MAX / ptr_size) {\n    // handle overflow, e.g. by returning an error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(ptr_type_size * ptr_size);\n    // continue with normal execution\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `INT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
