              "id": "glog-5806eab7-2ae9-4357-b079-aadd9039eba3",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of several variables (as in the provided code snippet), there is a risk of integer overflow. This can lead to the allocation of less memory than expected, which in turn can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of your multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done by comparing the result of the multiplication with the maximum value that can be held by the type of the variable used to store the result. If the result is greater, it means that an overflow has occurred and the program should handle this situation appropriately, for example by terminating with an error message.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t size = ...; // size of each element\nsize_t nnzb = ...; // number of elements\nsize_t bm = ...; // multiplier 1\nsize_t bn = ...; // multiplier 2\n\nif (nnzb > 0 && bm > 0 && bn > 0 && SIZE_MAX / nnzb / bm / bn < size) {\n    // handle overflow, e.g. by terminating the program\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n}\n\nvoid* ptr = malloc(size * nnzb * bm * bn);\n// continue with your code\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `climits` for `SIZE_MAX`\n- `cstdlib` for `malloc` and `exit`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
