              "id": "glog-1856038a-efce-448a-9cb3-9c9bcceb804d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a multiplication operation, there is a risk of integer overflow. If the multiplication overflows, the actual allocated size will be less than expected, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities when the program attempts to fill the allocated space.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur before performing the multiplication. This can be done by comparing the maximum possible value that can be held by the variable with the values being multiplied. If the multiplication would result in a value greater than the maximum, an overflow would occur and the operation should not be performed.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for potential overflow before performing the multiplication:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (ob > 0 && size > INT_MAX / ob) {\n    // handle error: multiplication would overflow\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(size * (ob + 1));\n    // continue with program\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `climits`: This library is used to get the maximum value that can be held by an integer.\n- `cstdlib`: This library is used for dynamic memory management functions like `malloc`.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
