            {
              "id": "glog-918492e2-5358-4db7-a04b-b809c6638309",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the multiplication `data_type_size * m * n` results in an integer overflow, the allocated memory block will be smaller than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability. This can lead to various security issues such as data corruption, crashes, and code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check for potential integer overflows when calculating the size of the memory to be allocated. This can be done by dividing the maximum possible size by the size of the data type and checking if the result is less than the number of elements you want to allocate.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (m > 0 && n > 0 && data_type_size <= UINT_MAX / m / n) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(data_type_size * m * n);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle error\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the multiplication would result in an integer overflow. If it would, we handle the error. If it wouldn't, we proceed with the `malloc` call.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `UINT_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerability Guide](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
