            {
              "id": "glog-e8ea568f-26b6-4c5b-9313-e36ce7187652",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. The vulnerability arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or calculated, leading to buffer overflow, integer overflow, or other memory corruption issues. In the given code snippet `malloc(data_type_size * m)`, if the value of `m` is not properly validated, it can lead to allocation of incorrect memory size, causing potential security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize the input values before using them in memory allocation functions like `malloc`.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc`, which initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n3. Consider using modern C++ features like smart pointers (`std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`) which automatically manage memory and help prevent memory leaks and other memory-related issues.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Assuming data_type_size is a constant\nconst size_t data_type_size = sizeof(int); // or any other data type\n\n// Validate m before using it\nif (m > 0 && m <= MAX_ALLOWED_SIZE) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(data_type_size * m);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    }\n    // Use ptr\n    free(ptr);\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library for the `malloc` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## CWE\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
