            {
              "id": "glog-a0b95a5a-3f80-44c5-94a8-8d53d495d583",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(size_t size)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory of the specified size. If the allocation is successful, it returns a pointer to the beginning of the block of memory. If the allocation fails, it returns a null pointer. \n\nA vulnerability can occur when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or is manipulated in such a way that it causes an overflow or underflow condition. This can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Always validate the size parameter before passing it to `malloc()`. Ensure that it is within the expected range and is not excessively large.\n2. Use functions that limit the amount of memory allocated, such as `calloc()`, which also initializes the allocated memory to zero.\n3. Handle the case where `malloc()` fails and returns a null pointer. This can prevent null pointer dereferences and potential crashes.\n4. Regularly use static code analysis tools to detect potential vulnerabilities related to dynamic memory allocation.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to properly use `malloc()`:\n\n```c++\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nvoid* safe_malloc(size_t size) {\n    if (size == 0 || size > MAX_SAFE_SIZE) {\n        // Handle error: invalid size\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error: allocation failed\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `MAX_SAFE_SIZE` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum safe size to allocate. This value will depend on your specific application and system.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library (`stdlib.h`), so no additional libraries are needed.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
