            {
              "id": "glog-c66c49c3-f4b4-4218-8c77-f8725b9c53d0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are known to be dangerous due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues. These functions are often prohibited in secure coding standards.\n\nThe `getenv` function is one such function. It is used to retrieve the value of an environment variable from the process environment. The problem with `getenv` is that it returns a pointer to a string that may be overwritten by subsequent calls to `getenv`, `setenv`, or `unsetenv`. This can lead to race conditions if the application is multithreaded.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using `getenv` in multithreaded applications. If you need to use environment variables, consider copying the values into your own buffers to avoid race conditions.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `getenv`, you can use `secure_getenv` if it's available on your platform. `secure_getenv` behaves the same as `getenv` but returns NULL if the process is being run with elevated privileges. This can help prevent privilege escalation attacks.\n\nHere's an example of how you can use `secure_getenv`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nconst char* value = secure_getenv(\"MY_VARIABLE\");\nif (value != NULL) {\n    // Use the value\n} else {\n    // Handle the error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `getenv` and `secure_getenv` functions are part of the C standard library, so you don't need any additional libraries to use them.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-5499cba9-a1e7-4798-aeb5-5a5e16f58846",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc()` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The vulnerability arises when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or controlled, which can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always validate and control the size parameter passed to `malloc()`. This includes checking for integer overflows and underflows, ensuring the size is not zero, and ensuring the size does not exceed the available memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to validate the size parameter before passing it to `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (that.size() > 0 && that.size() <= SIZE_MAX) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(that.size());\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    } else {\n        // Continue with program\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `that.size()` is checked to ensure it is greater than zero and does not exceed the maximum size that can be passed to `malloc()`. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory, it returns `NULL` and the failure is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS161085"
                ]
              }
            },
