              "id": "glog-11938cec-0df0-4a91-89d6-8bbe5aa563a4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc()` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory of a specified size. The vulnerability arises when the size parameter passed to `malloc()` is not properly validated or controlled, which can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always validate and control the size parameter passed to `malloc()`. This includes checking for integer overflows and underflows, ensuring the size is not zero, and ensuring the size does not exceed the available memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to validate the size parameter before passing it to `malloc()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (that.size() > 0 && that.size() <= SIZE_MAX) {\n    void* ptr = malloc(that.size());\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n    } else {\n        // Continue with program\n    }\n} else {\n    // Handle invalid size\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, `that.size()` is checked to ensure it is greater than zero and does not exceed the maximum size that can be passed to `malloc()`. If `malloc()` fails to allocate the requested memory, it returns `NULL` and the failure is handled appropriately.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
