            {
              "id": "glog-3d691858-3f77-4b31-bbac-b12e87dc25d4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer over-read, information leakage, or even a segmentation fault.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(rocblas_tensile_commit_hash[0])`, the vulnerability arises if `rocblas_tensile_commit_hash[0]` is not a null-terminated string. This could lead to undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Alternatively, use safer functions that take the size of the buffer as an argument, such as `strnlen()`, which will not read past the specified length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nAssuming `rocblas_tensile_commit_hash` is an array of strings, a safer way to get the length of the first string would be:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t len = strnlen(rocblas_tensile_commit_hash[0], MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\nWhere `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum length of the string that you expect. This will prevent `strnlen()` from reading past the end of the buffer.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional libraries are needed to use it. However, if `rocblas_tensile_commit_hash` is part of the ROCm software platform, you would need to include the appropriate ROCm headers and link against the ROCm libraries.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
