              "id": "glog-b6212037-3423-46fc-bef2-1ba7844b756a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strncat`, `snprintf`, or `strlcpy` (note that `strlcpy` is not a standard C library function). \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with a safer function, such as `strncpy`. Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar name[13]; // size of \"rocblas-bench\" + 1 for null terminator\nstrncpy(name, \"rocblas-bench\", sizeof(name));\nname[sizeof(name) - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library (`<cstring>` in C++) to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
