              "id": "glog-e3bab6e4-0e31-4973-a3ac-522621f2020f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detection of Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming arises when certain C functions, known for their potential to cause security issues, are used inappropriately. The `gmtime` function is one such function that can lead to vulnerabilities. It converts a time value to a `tm` structure representing Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). However, `gmtime` is not thread-safe because it returns a pointer to a statically allocated `tm` structure, which can be overwritten by subsequent calls to `gmtime` or other time-related functions.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate the risks associated with using `gmtime`, consider the following strategies:\n\n1. **Use Thread-Safe Alternatives**: Prefer using `gmtime_r` or `gmtime_s`, which are thread-safe alternatives to `gmtime`.\n2. **Avoid Global State**: Minimize reliance on global state that can be modified by other parts of the program.\n3. **Encapsulation**: Encapsulate time-related operations within a class or function to control access and modifications.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is an example of how to replace `gmtime` with `gmtime_r` to ensure thread safety:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <ctime>\n#include <mutex>\n\n// Function to safely convert time_t to tm structure\nvoid safeGmtime(const time_t* time, struct tm* result) {\n    // Use gmtime_r for thread safety\n    gmtime_r(time, result);\n}\n\nint main() {\n    time_t rawtime;\n    struct tm timeinfo;\n    \n    // Get the current time\n    time(&rawtime);\n    \n    // Convert to UTC using thread-safe function\n    safeGmtime(&rawtime, &timeinfo);\n    \n    // Output the result\n    std::cout << \"Current UTC time: \" << asctime(&timeinfo);\n    \n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, the following standard libraries are required:\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output stream operations.\n- `<ctime>`: For time-related functions and structures.\n- `<mutex>`: For thread synchronization (if needed in more complex scenarios).\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices - Quick Reference Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [OWASP Top Ten](https://owasp.org/www-project-top-ten/)\n\n### Common Weakness Enumeration\n\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
