              "id": "glog-ed92129c-051b-4d35-a134-30f9f1ac8bce",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. `malloc` is a function that dynamically allocates memory at runtime. The problem arises when the size of the memory to be allocated is not properly validated or controlled, which can lead to various security issues such as buffer overflows, memory corruption, or denial of service attacks.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always validate and control the size of the memory to be allocated. This can be done by checking the size before calling `malloc` and ensuring it is within a reasonable and expected range. Additionally, always check the return value of `malloc` to ensure that the memory allocation was successful. If `malloc` fails, it returns a null pointer, and any attempt to use this pointer will result in undefined behavior.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to properly use `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\n// Define a reasonable maximum size.\n#define MAX_SIZE 1000\n\nvoid* allocate(size_t size) {\n    if (size > MAX_SIZE) {\n        // The requested size is too large.\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    void* ptr = malloc(size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // The memory allocation failed.\n        return NULL;\n    }\n\n    return ptr;\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, the size of the memory to be allocated is checked before calling `malloc`, and the return value of `malloc` is checked before the pointer is used.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc` function is part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-131: Incorrect Calculation of Buffer Size](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/131.html)"
