            {
              "id": "glog-9cc5e971-8b6f-4d2d-a902-a17c7aea338c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can cause a program to crash or, in the worst case, allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. These include `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`. However, these functions also have their own issues and may not be available on all platforms. \n\nAnother option is to use higher-level string handling facilities provided by C++, such as the `std::string` class, which automatically manages memory and helps prevent buffer overflows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with `strncpy`:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_mlbsr.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename) - 1);\nthis->filename[sizeof(this->filename) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nOr, if `strlcpy` is available:\n\n```cpp\nstrlcpy(this->filename, this->b_mlbsr.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename));\n```\n\nOr, use `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\nthis->filename = this->b_mlbsr;\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `cstring` or `string.h` for `strcpy`, `strncpy`, or `strlcpy`\n- `string` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-1d44e924-405b-48be-9f1c-24068f406bc4",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string from source to destination. It is considered unsafe because it does not check the array bounds of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is longer than the destination buffer.\n\nBuffer overflow vulnerabilities can lead to various security issues, including arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or data corruption.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should avoid using `strcpy` and instead use safer alternatives that check the array bounds of the destination buffer. These include functions like `strncpy`, `strlcpy`, or `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // for strncpy\n#include <string>  // for std::string\n\n// Assuming filename is a char array with known size MAX_SIZE\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_mlcsr.c_str(), MAX_SIZE - 1);\nthis->filename[MAX_SIZE - 1] = '\\0'; // Ensure null-termination\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncpy` is used instead of `strcpy`. It copies up to `MAX_SIZE - 1` characters from the source string to the destination buffer, and then manually null-terminates the destination string to ensure it is a valid string.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>` for `strncpy`\n- `<string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS185832"
