            {
              "id": "glog-9143dbd8-740a-453d-9729-82e3f297c7ab",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy` is used to copy a string from `this->b_matrixmarket` to `this->filename`. If `this->b_matrixmarket` is larger than `this->filename`, it can cause a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. In C++, this can be done using the `strncpy` function, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to be copied. However, `strncpy` does not null-terminate the destination string if the source string is longer than the specified length, so you must manually add a null terminator.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_matrixmarket.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename) - 1);\nthis->filename[sizeof(this->filename) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy at most `sizeof(this->filename) - 1` characters from `this->b_matrixmarket` to `this->filename`, and then manually add a null terminator to `this->filename`.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for the `strcpy` and `strncpy` functions\n- `<string>`: for the `std::string` class and its `c_str` method\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
