              "id": "glog-91fddb24-c21b-4268-a5ab-4eb82967b6cd",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcpy` function in C++ is a standard library function that copies a string. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not check the size of the destination buffer, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities if the source string is larger than the destination buffer. This can lead to arbitrary code execution, denial of service, or information disclosure vulnerabilities.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strcpy(this->filename, this->b_rocalution.c_str())`, the `strcpy` function is used to copy the string from `this->b_rocalution` to `this->filename`. If `this->b_rocalution` is larger than `this->filename`, it can cause a buffer overflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string copy functions that check the size of the destination buffer. In C++, this can be done using the `strncpy` function, which takes an additional parameter specifying the maximum number of characters to be copied.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcpy` function with the `strncpy` function:\n\n```cpp\nstrncpy(this->filename, this->b_rocalution.c_str(), sizeof(this->filename) - 1);\nthis->filename[sizeof(this->filename) - 1] = '\\0';\n```\n\nThis code will copy at most `sizeof(this->filename) - 1` characters from `this->b_rocalution` to `this->filename`, and then manually null-terminate the string to ensure it is properly terminated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<cstring>`: for the `strncpy` function\n- `<string>`: for the `std::string` class and its `c_str` method\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are valid and active for anonymous users at the time of writing."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
