                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc(size1)` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate a block of memory of `size1` bytes and returns a pointer to the first byte of the allocated space. The program can then use this space to store data. The vulnerability arises when the `malloc(size1)` function is used improperly, leading to memory leaks, buffer overflows, or other memory corruption issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to always check the return value of `malloc(size1)`. If the function fails to allocate the requested block of memory, it returns a null pointer. Ignoring this can lead to null pointer dereferencing which can crash the program.\n\nAlso, always ensure to free the allocated memory after use to prevent memory leaks. Not doing so can lead to exhaustion of memory resources.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple example of how to properly use `malloc(size1)`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nint* allocateArray(int size) {\n    int* array = (int*) malloc(size * sizeof(int));\n    if (array == NULL) {\n        // Handle malloc failure\n        // ...\n    }\n    return array;\n}\n\nvoid useArray() {\n    int* array = allocateArray(100);\n    // Use array...\n    // ...\n    // Don't forget to free the memory\n    free(array);\n}\n```\n\nIn this example, we check if `malloc(size1)` returns a null pointer and handle the failure accordingly. We also ensure to free the allocated memory after use.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `malloc(size1)` function is part of the `stdlib.h` library in C++. Therefore, to use this function, you need to include this library in your code.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-401: Missing Release of Memory after Effective Lifetime](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/401.html)"
