            {
              "id": "glog-920320b7-b2d5-4108-90d6-f361adb9bb27",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables (like `count * sizeof(void*)`), there is a risk of integer overflow. If the multiplication overflows, `malloc` will be called with a smaller size than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check if an integer overflow can occur when calculating the size for `malloc`. This can be done by dividing the maximum possible value that can be held by the size variable (`SIZE_MAX`) by one of the operands. If the result is less than the other operand, then an overflow would occur.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdint.h>\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (count > SIZE_MAX/sizeof(void*)) {\n    /* Handle error */\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(count * sizeof(void*));\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        /* Handle error */\n    }\n    /* Continue with program */\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- stdint.h\n- stdlib.h\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
