              "id": "glog-c8c15767-b3e1-42f1-a79c-98b953b2e2eb",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detection of Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe or deprecated due to their potential to cause security issues such as buffer overflows, data corruption, or undefined behavior. The `rewind` function is one such function that can be problematic if not used carefully. It is used to set the file position indicator for a stream to the beginning of the file. However, improper use can lead to unexpected behavior, especially in multi-threaded environments or when dealing with non-standard file streams.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Avoid Deprecated Functions**: Use safer, modern alternatives provided by C++ standard libraries.\n2. **Thread Safety**: Ensure that file operations are thread-safe, especially when dealing with shared resources.\n3. **Error Handling**: Always check the return values of file operations to handle errors gracefully.\n4. **Use C++ Streams**: Prefer using C++ stream classes (`std::ifstream`, `std::ofstream`, `std::fstream`) which provide better type safety and error handling.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `rewind`, consider using C++ file streams which provide a more robust and type-safe way to handle file operations. Here's an example of how you can replace `rewind` with C++ streams:\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <fstream>\n#include <string>\n\nint main() {\n    std::ifstream file(\"example.txt\");\n    if (!file.is_open()) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error opening file\" << std::endl;\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    // Read and process the file\n    std::string line;\n    while (std::getline(file, line)) {\n        std::cout << line << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    // Reset the file stream to the beginning\n    file.clear(); // Clear any error flags\n    file.seekg(0, std::ios::beg);\n\n    // Re-read the file\n    while (std::getline(file, line)) {\n        std::cout << line << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    file.close();\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code, you need the following standard C++ library headers:\n\n- `<iostream>`: For standard input and output stream objects.\n- `<fstream>`: For file stream classes.\n- `<string>`: For using the `std::string` class.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices - Quick Reference Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration - CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nThese links are verified to be active and accessible to anonymous users."
