              "id": "glog-75888b66-cb96-4f45-8bc0-038492b7923e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code or cause a denial of service.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `strcat(dir,'Q')`, the function attempts to append the string 'Q' to the string `dir`. If `dir` does not have enough space to hold the additional characters, a buffer overflow occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a safer version of the code using `strncat`:\n\n```cpp\nchar dir[SIZE];\n// ... (initialize dir)\nstrncat(dir, \"Q\", SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this code, `SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1` ensures that `strncat` does not append more characters than `dir` can hold, preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code requires the `<cstring>` library for the `strncat` and `strlen` functions.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links are active and accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
