                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. This can allow an attacker to overwrite adjacent memory and execute arbitrary code. The specific vulnerability sink here is `strcat(dir,'P')`.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`. This function takes an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` as follows:\n\n```cpp\nchar dir[SIZE];\n// ...\nstrncat(dir, \"P\", SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nThis code will append the string \"P\" to `dir`, but not more than `SIZE - strlen(dir) - 1` characters, leaving room for the null terminator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat` and `strncat` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)"
