              "id": "glog-0c7e6da6-6f3b-4e98-8742-6933122aaede",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. If an attacker can influence the data that is being concatenated, they can cause the program to crash or potentially execute arbitrary code.\n\nIn the provided code `strcat(dir,'B_30')`, the function attempts to append the string 'B_30' to the string pointed to by `dir`. If `dir` does not have enough space to hold the additional characters, a buffer overflow occurs.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking. In C++, this can be achieved by using the `std::string` class or the `strncat` function.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `strcat` function with `strncat` or use `std::string`. Here is an example of how to do it:\n\n```cpp\nchar dir[100];\nstrncpy(dir, \"some_directory\", sizeof(dir));\nstrncat(dir, \"B_30\", sizeof(dir) - strlen(dir) - 1);\n```\n\nOr using `std::string`:\n\n```cpp\nstd::string dir = \"some_directory\";\ndir += \"B_30\";\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `#include <cstring>` for `strncpy` and `strncat`\n- `#include <string>` for `std::string`\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)"
