            {
              "id": "glog-291159d5-74c3-498e-a9bf-41b89f65eab2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detection of Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming arises when certain C functions, known for their potential to cause security issues, are used inappropriately. One such function is `fscanf`, which can lead to buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and other security issues if not used carefully. This vulnerability occurs when `fscanf` is used without proper input validation and bounds checking, allowing an attacker to manipulate input data to exploit the program.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Always validate input data before processing it. Ensure that the input conforms to expected formats and ranges.\n2. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Consider using safer alternatives like `fgets` followed by `sscanf`, which allows for better control over input size and format.\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Implement bounds checking to ensure that input data does not exceed buffer sizes.\n4. **Error Handling**: Implement robust error handling to manage unexpected input or errors during input processing.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nBelow is an example of how to replace `fscanf` with a safer alternative:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n#include <cstring>\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    FILE *file = fopen(\"input.txt\", \"r\");\n    if (file == nullptr) {\n        std::cerr << \"Error opening file\" << std::endl;\n        return 1;\n    }\n\n    char buffer[100];\n    if (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer), file) != nullptr) {\n        int value;\n        if (sscanf(buffer, \"%d\", &value) == 1) {\n            std::cout << \"Read value: \" << value << std::endl;\n        } else {\n            std::cerr << \"Error reading integer from buffer\" << std::endl;\n        }\n    } else {\n        std::cerr << \"Error reading from file\" << std::endl;\n    }\n\n    fclose(file);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the above code example, the following standard libraries are required:\n\n- `<cstdio>`: For file operations and standard input/output functions.\n- `<cstring>`: For string manipulation functions.\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output stream operations.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices - Quick Reference Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE-676): Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nThese links were verified to be active and accessible to anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "glog-8cfe2dcf-6dac-48c1-94e6-12cca32dacb2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "### Description\n\nThe \"Detection of Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability in C++ programming arises when unsafe C functions, such as `sscanf`, are used. These functions can lead to buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, and other security issues if not used carefully. `sscanf` is particularly risky because it does not perform bounds checking on the input buffer, which can lead to buffer overflows if the input is larger than expected.\n\n### General Mitigation Advice\n\n1. **Use Safer Alternatives**: Prefer using safer functions like `snprintf` or C++ streams (`std::stringstream`) that provide bounds checking and type safety.\n2. **Input Validation**: Always validate input data before processing it.\n3. **Bounds Checking**: Ensure that buffers are large enough to hold the expected input and that the input is properly null-terminated.\n4. **Compiler Warnings**: Enable compiler warnings to detect unsafe function usage.\n5. **Static Analysis Tools**: Use static analysis tools to detect and refactor unsafe code patterns.\n\n### Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\n#### Vulnerable Code Example\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdio>\n\nvoid processInput(const char* input) {\n    char buffer[10];\n    sscanf(input, \"%s\", buffer); // Vulnerable to buffer overflow\n}\n```\n\n#### Fixed Code Example\n\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <sstream>\n#include <string>\n\nvoid processInput(const std::string& input) {\n    std::istringstream iss(input);\n    std::string buffer;\n    iss >> buffer; // Safer alternative using C++ streams\n    if (buffer.length() > 9) {\n        std::cerr << \"Input too long!\" << std::endl;\n        return;\n    }\n    // Process buffer safely\n}\n```\n\n### Library Dependencies\n\nTo execute the fixed code example, the following standard C++ libraries are required:\n\n- `<iostream>`: For input and output stream operations.\n- `<sstream>`: For string stream operations.\n- `<string>`: For using the `std::string` class.\n\n### Relevant Resources\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices - Quick Reference Guide](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [Common Weakness Enumeration - CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nThese links are verified to be active and accessible for anonymous users."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
