              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strcat` function in C++ is used to concatenate strings. However, it is considered unsafe because it does not perform bounds checking, and thus can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities. In the provided code snippet, `strcat(\"ind\",matname)`, the function attempts to append the `matname` string to the string literal `\"ind\"`. This is problematic because string literals are read-only and have a fixed size, which can lead to a buffer overflow if `matname` is larger than the remaining space in the string literal.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that perform bounds checking, such as `strncat`, `strlcat`, or `snprintf`. These functions take an additional parameter that specifies the maximum number of characters to be appended, thus preventing buffer overflow.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\nchar dest[50] = \"ind\";\nstrncat(dest, matname, sizeof(dest) - strlen(dest) - 1);\n```\n\nIn this code, `strncat` is used instead of `strcat`. The third parameter is the maximum number of characters to be appended from `matname`, which is calculated as the size of `dest` minus the current length of `dest` minus 1 (for the null terminator).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strcat` and `strncat` functions are part of the C standard library, so no additional library dependencies are required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
