              "id": "glog-6dd1eb91-c810-4888-a201-88b8109be99c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `memcpy` function in C++ is used to copy a block of memory from one location to another. This function is vulnerable to buffer overflow attacks if the size of the data being copied is larger than the size of the destination buffer. In the provided code snippet, if `len` is larger than the size of `_charBuffer`, it can lead to a buffer overflow, which can cause a program crash or potentially allow an attacker to execute arbitrary code.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always ensure that the size of the data being copied does not exceed the size of the destination buffer. This can be done by checking the size of the data and the size of the buffer before calling `memcpy`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring> // for memcpy and strlen\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t bufferSize = sizeof(_charBuffer);\nif (len <= bufferSize) {\n    memcpy( _charBuffer, p, len );\n} else {\n    // Handle error: data is too large to fit in the buffer\n}\n```\n\nIn this fixed code, we first check if `len` is less than or equal to the size of `_charBuffer`. If it is, we proceed with the `memcpy`. If it is not, we handle the error in an appropriate way for the context of the program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `memcpy` function is part of the `<cstring>` library in C++. Therefore, the `<cstring>` library must be included in order for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Buffer Overflow Attack](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Buffer_overflow_attack)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)"
