                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C and C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`'\\0'`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, potentially leading to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, always ensure that your strings are null-terminated. Avoid using `strlen()` on strings that may not be null-terminated. Instead, consider using functions that take the length of the string as an argument, such as `strnlen()`, which will not read past a specified length.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `strlen(endTag)`, use `strnlen(endTag, MAX_LENGTH)`, where `MAX_LENGTH` is the maximum expected length of `endTag`.\n\n```cpp\n#include <string.h> // for strnlen\n\nconst size_t MAX_LENGTH = 100; // replace with your maximum expected length\nsize_t length = strnlen(endTag, MAX_LENGTH);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<string.h>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-126: Buffer Over-read](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/126.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
