              "id": "glog-cc4b24e2-cd1c-42a3-9820-c941b8d24ec8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables, and one or both of these variables are controlled by the user, an attacker could potentially cause an integer overflow. This could lead to the allocation of less memory than expected, which in turn could lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust data provided by the user. In addition, you should also check for integer overflows before performing operations that could potentially overflow. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nA possible fix for this vulnerability would be to check for an integer overflow before calling `malloc`. Here is an example of how you could do this:\n\n```cpp\nif (m_cMaxElements > SIZE_MAX / TypeInfo<CType>::ElementSize) {\n    // handle overflow error\n} else {\n    malloc(TypeInfo<CType>::ElementSize * m_cMaxElements);\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `SIZE_MAX` is the maximum value that can be represented by `size_t`, the type of the argument to `malloc`. If `m_cMaxElements` is greater than `SIZE_MAX / TypeInfo<CType>::ElementSize`, then multiplying `TypeInfo<CType>::ElementSize` by `m_cMaxElements` would result in an overflow.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not appear to require any specific library dependencies beyond the standard C++ libraries.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
              },
