            {
              "id": "glog-3c8382e7-8ab8-4075-90b5-0bddb74a02d2",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a multiplication, as in the provided code snippet, and one of the multiplicands is controlled by the user or can be influenced by an attacker, an integer overflow can occur. This can lead to a smaller block of memory being allocated than intended. If the program then tries to write more data to this block than it can hold, a buffer overflow occurs, which can lead to arbitrary code execution, crashes, or other undefined behavior.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of a multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done using built-in functions in some compilers, or by manually checking if the result is less than either of the multiplicands. Additionally, always check the result of `malloc` to make sure that the memory allocation was successful.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow and the result of `malloc`:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t size = TypeInfo<DType>::ElementSize;\nif (m_dMaxElements > SIZE_MAX / size) {\n    // handle error\n}\nvoid* ptr = malloc(size * m_dMaxElements);\nif (!ptr) {\n    // handle error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not give enough context to determine any library dependencies. However, `malloc` is part of the C standard library, so `<cstdlib>` or `<stdlib.h>` would need to be included.\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
