            {
              "id": "glog-29b38425-f9d9-403d-926c-245aacb6aca9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables, and one or both of these variables are controlled by a user, this can lead to an integer overflow. If the overflow occurs, the allocated memory block will be smaller than expected, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done by comparing the result of the multiplication to the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the result. If the result is larger, an overflow has occurred and the `malloc` call should not be made.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\n// ...\n\nsize_t size = TypeInfo<BType>::ElementSize;\nif (m_bMaxElements > (SIZE_MAX / size)) {\n    // handle overflow, e.g., by returning an error or throwing an exception\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(size * m_bMaxElements);\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<climits>`: for `SIZE_MAX`\n- `<cstdlib>`: for `malloc`\n\n## OWASP Resources\n\n- [OWASP C/C++ Vulnerabilities](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_Quick_Reference_Guide_v2.pdf)\n\n## Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE)\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
