            {
              "id": "glog-b6f26c39-8661-4bf9-91cf-4406f909699b",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a result of a multiplication, like `count * sizeof(T)`, and the multiplication results in an integer overflow, the allocated memory block will be smaller than expected. This can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities, as the program might try to write more data to the block than it can hold.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always check the result of the multiplication for overflow before passing it to `malloc`. This can be done by comparing the result of the multiplication to the maximum value that can be held by the data type of the result. If the result is larger, an overflow has occurred and the program should handle this error appropriately.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple way to check for overflow:\n\n```cpp\n#include <climits>\n#include <cstdlib>\n\nif (count > 0 && sizeof(T) > UINT_MAX / count) {\n    // handle overflow error\n} else {\n    T* ptr = (T*) malloc(count * sizeof(T));\n    // continue with program\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following libraries:\n\n- `climits`: for `UINT_MAX`\n- `cstdlib`: for `malloc`\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
