            {
              "id": "glog-d342d3ec-43e7-4163-84dd-be76c92a7535",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability in question is related to the `malloc` function in C++. This function is used to allocate a block of memory dynamically. The size of this block is determined by the argument passed to the function. If the argument is a product of two variables, and one or both of these variables are controlled by an attacker, it can lead to an integer overflow. This overflow can cause the allocated memory to be smaller than expected, leading to a buffer overflow vulnerability when this memory is used.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always validate and limit the size of the memory you are trying to allocate. This can be done by checking the values of `TypeInfo<AType>::ElementSize` and `m_aMaxElements` before using them in the `malloc` function. You should also consider using functions that can handle memory allocation errors more gracefully, such as `calloc`, which zeroes out the allocated memory, or `realloc`, which can adjust the size of the allocated memory.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\nsize_t elementSize = TypeInfo<AType>::ElementSize;\nsize_t maxElements = m_aMaxElements;\n\nif (elementSize && maxElements > SIZE_MAX / elementSize) {\n    // handle error: the calculation would overflow\n} else {\n    void* buffer = malloc(elementSize * maxElements);\n    if (!buffer) {\n        // handle error: malloc failed\n    }\n    // continue with program, buffer is safely allocated\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we first check if the multiplication would overflow. If it would, we handle the error. If it wouldn't, we proceed to call `malloc`. After calling `malloc`, we check if it returned `NULL`, which would indicate that the allocation failed. If it did fail, we handle the error. If it didn't, we continue with the program.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example does not appear to require any specific library dependencies beyond the standard C++ libraries.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Links\n\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)\n- [CWE-680: Integer Overflow to Buffer Overflow](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/680.html)"
